<h2>The allegations</h2>

<p>FIR No. 132/2021, registered by the Crime Branch, invoked Sections 3 and 9 of the Official Secrets Act, 1923, read with Sections 409, 201, 380, 381, 457, 120B and 34 of the IPC. The prosecution alleged that classified military documents — touching military training, the Armed Forces Special Operations Division and intelligence material — were recovered from the applicant, and that the Army verified them as genuine and sensitive. He was alleged to have acted as an intermediary between a serving Naik Clerk and an official of the Pakistan High Commission, with the chain supported by call detail records, financial transactions and the statement of his own employee, who is said to have delivered documents to the High Commission on his instructions. The applicant was arrested in July 2021; charges have been framed and prosecution evidence has begun.</p>

<h2>The bail plea and the court's answers</h2>

<p>The application under Section 483 of the BNSS pressed four grounds. The order, decided on 25 September 2026, deals with each on its own terms rather than disposing of the plea on the gravity of the label alone.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Ground urged</th><th>The court's treatment</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Custody of about three years and four months, with compliant conduct on earlier interim bail</td><td>Weighed in the applicant's favour, along with satisfactory jail conduct, but found insufficient against the prima facie material</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Weak evidence resting on disclosures</td><td>Rejected: the recovery of Army-verified classified documents, call records, financial trails and the employee's statement meant the case did not rest on disclosure statements alone</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Delay in trial</td><td>Charges framed and evidence underway; without complete trial-court order sheets, responsibility for delay could not fairly be assigned</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical condition — wheelchair dependence, multiple ailments</td><td>Noted with care, but the medical report did not state that the prescribed treatment could not be given in custody</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Dismissing the application, the court coupled the refusal with protective directions: the trial court must expedite prosecution evidence and avoid unnecessary adjournments, and the Jail Superintendent must ensure timely medical care, with any investigation or treatment unavailable within the jail to be arranged at an appropriate government hospital.</p>

<h2>The principle worth noting</h2>

<p>The most quotable proposition in the order cuts against reflexive assumptions: national security allegations demand close scrutiny, but they do not oust bail jurisdiction, and the statutory maximum of fourteen years does not by itself decide the application. The court made only a prima facie assessment, expressly avoiding the kind of evidentiary evaluation that belongs to trial. That discipline runs both ways — it prevents the prosecution from winning on rhetoric, and it prevents the defence from converting a bail hearing into a premature acquittal argument.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p><strong>What this order signals for OSA and espionage bail pleas:</strong></p>
  <p>The label on the FIR does not end the enquiry — courts will look at the actual material.</p>
  <p>Recoveries verified by the concerned service, financial trails and witness statements weigh far more than disclosure statements alone.</p>
  <p>Medical grounds need a report saying custody-based treatment is inadequate, not merely that the applicant is unwell.</p>
  <p>Where bail is refused after long custody, expedition directions follow — and a stagnating trial can become the changed circumstance for a renewed plea.</p>
</div>

<h2>Context for practitioners</h2>

<p>The Official Secrets Act survived the 2023-24 recodification of criminal law: the IPC, CrPC and Evidence Act gave way to the BNS, BNSS and BSA on 1 July 2024, but the OSA continues as a special statute, now read with the new procedural code — hence the present application under Section 483 BNSS (formerly Section 439 CrPC). Offences in this FIR, registered in 2021, continue under the IPC. Bail practice in such cases is therefore ordinary in form and extraordinary in substance: the usual triad of flight risk, tampering and gravity applies, but the gravity limb carries the full weight of the security allegations, and realistic defence strategy focuses on custody duration, trial pace and health — grounds this order shows the court will take seriously, even while refusing relief on the facts.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court bail order for general information. Observations at the bail stage are prima facie and decide nothing about guilt. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
